{"author":"schacon@gmail.com","body":"Attachments would let users associate files (images, logs, patches, etc.) with a ticket. Rather than storing blob data directly in git-meta, attachments are stored as prefix references — pointers to where the blob lives in another system.\n\n**Data model:**\n- `attachments` List field on tickets\n- Each entry: `{name, prefix, sha, mime_type, size, added_by, added_at}`\n- `prefix` is a namespace/location hint (e.g. `gh:owner/repo`, `s3:bucket/path`, `git:sha`, `url:https://...`)\n- `sha` is the content hash for deduplication/verification\n\n**Example commands:**\n- `ti attach <file> --ticket abc` — upload/register an attachment\n- `ti attach --url https://... --ticket abc` — attach by URL reference\n- `ti attach --list --ticket abc` — list attachments\n- `ti attach --remove <name> --ticket abc` — remove an attachment\n\n**Prefix types to consider:**\n- `git:<sha>` — blob exists in the git repo's object store\n- `url:<url>` — external HTTP resource\n- `gh:<owner/repo/blob>` — GitHub-hosted file\n- `local:<path>` — local filesystem (for personal use)\n\nThe prefix approach keeps git-meta lightweight while allowing flexible storage backends. Rendering tools can resolve prefixes to fetch/display the actual content."}